        </div>
      </div>
    </header>
    
    <main class="pt-24 pb-16">
      <div class="container mx-auto px-4 max-w-4xl">
        <h1 class="text-4xl md:text-5xl font-bold text-foreground mb-8">Tennis Elbow Treatment in North Vancouver</h1>
        
        <h2 class="text-3xl font-bold text-foreground mb-6">What is tennis elbow?</h2>
        
        <p class="text-lg text-muted-foreground mb-4">Tennis elbow is a tendon problem on the outside of the elbow — now understood as a degenerative tendinopathy (wear and failed healing in the tendon), not active inflammation — usually from repeated gripping or wrist movements. You don't have to play tennis to get it. Most cases improve, but recovery is often slower than people expect, and it tends to recur if load capacity isn't restored through progressive exercise rather than just rested.</p>
        
        <p class="text-lg text-muted-foreground mb-4">You don't need to play tennis to get tennis elbow. Gripping a coffee mug, turning a doorknob, shaking someone's hand, typing — any gripping or twisting motion can send a sharp pain through the outside of your elbow and down your forearm. It starts as a mild annoyance and escalates until you're dropping things and modifying how you use your hand.</p>

        <p class="text-lg text-muted-foreground mb-12">Tennis elbow (lateral elbow tendinopathy, also called lateral epicondylalgia) is one of the most common repetitive-strain problems — and one of the most frustrating to resolve when treatment stays focused only on the tendon. At Scandinavian Clinic, Eva Andersson, RMT, uses clinical Registered Massage Therapy to look beyond the elbow and treat the wider chain involved.</p>

        <div class="prose prose-lg max-w-none text-muted-foreground">
          
          <div class="my-8">
            <a href="https://booking.scandinavianclinic.com" class="booking-link inline-block bg-accent hover:bg-accent/90 text-accent-foreground font-medium py-3 px-6 rounded-md transition-colors mr-4">Book Your Appointment</a>
            <a href="https://scandinavianclinic.com" class="inline-block text-primary font-medium py-3 hover:underline">See all conditions Eva treats</a>
          </div>

          <hr class="my-12 border-border" />

          <h2 class="text-3xl font-bold text-foreground mb-6">Understanding Tennis Elbow</h2>
          <h3 class="text-2xl font-semibold text-foreground mb-4">More than a tendon problem</h3>

          <img src={imgHero} alt="Pain at the lateral epicondyle radiating down the forearm extensors" class="w-full max-w-2xl mx-auto rounded-lg shadow-md my-10" />

          <p>Tennis elbow involves the tendons of the forearm extensor muscles where they attach to the bony bump on the outside of the elbow (the lateral epicondyle). These muscles control wrist extension and grip — they work every time you lift, grip, type, or twist your forearm.</p>
          
          <p>The older view treated tennis elbow as simple inflammation to be rested away. It's now understood as a degenerative tendinopathy — a failed-healing process in the tendon (angiofibroblastic tendinosis), not an actively inflamed one — which is part of why rest alone so often disappoints. And from a whole-body (Prague School) perspective, pain at the lateral epicondyle is rarely a purely local problem: it is often the structural endpoint of a chain that overloads the forearm. Two contributors are commonly missed:</p>
          
          <ul class="space-y-4">
            <li><strong>The nerve factor.</strong> The radial nerve runs from the neck through the arm to the hand, passing close to the extensor muscles near the lateral epicondyle (around the supinator arch / arcade of Frohse). When it becomes sensitised or mechanically restricted, it can create pain that looks and feels like tendon pain — and nerve involvement is a recognised part of the differential diagnosis of lateral elbow pain (Vavken, Praxis, 2017). This kind of entrapment is often secondary to soft-tissue tightness driven by how the arm is being controlled, rather than a primary local fault — so it's addressed alongside restoring movement, not by nerve gliding on its own.</li>
            <li><strong>The chain above the elbow.</strong> The radial nerve originates in the cervical spine, and how you stabilise higher up matters. Faulty trunk stabilisation — for example a hyperlordotic or chest-breathing pattern — can lead to poor centration of the shoulder blade, which forces the forearm extensors to work as secondary stabilisers during gripping. Neck stiffness, shoulder-blade mechanics (scapular dyskinesis), and posture are therefore common contributing factors — not necessarily the single primary cause, but dynamic drivers that keep the elbow overloaded.</li>
          </ul>

          <h4 class="text-xl font-bold text-foreground mt-8 mb-4">Common symptoms:</h4>
          <ul class="space-y-2">
            <li>Pain on the outside of the elbow, especially when gripping or lifting with the palm facing down</li>
            <li>Weak grip — dropping objects, difficulty opening jars or bottles</li>
            <li>Pain that worsens with repetitive wrist movements — typing, using tools, cooking</li>
            <li>Tenderness directly over the bony point on the outside of the elbow</li>
            <li>Forearm aching or tightness extending toward the wrist</li>
            <li>Symptoms that improve with rest but return on activity</li>
          </ul>

          <p class="font-medium">When to seek treatment: if elbow pain has lasted more than three to four weeks despite rest, if a brace hasn't helped, or if grip weakness is affecting daily activities or work, an assessment can clarify whether the issue is tendon, nerve, chain-related, or a mix — which determines the right treatment.</p>

          <img src={imgIllustration} alt="Chain diagram: Trunk to Scapula to Elbow" class="w-full max-w-2xl mx-auto rounded-lg shadow-md my-10 bg-white" />

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-semibold text-foreground mb-4">Why is my tennis elbow taking so long to heal?</h3>
          <p>This is one of the most common frustrations with tennis elbow, and it deserves an honest answer. General health resources often describe recovery in weeks to a few months, but real recovery — especially for cases that were present a while before treatment started — commonly takes several months, and sometimes up to a year or more for established cases. This isn't a sign something has gone wrong: tendons heal and remodel slowly because they have a limited blood supply, and a degenerative tendon needs time and the right load to rebuild. Knowing this upfront helps set realistic expectations and avoid the discouragement of expecting a two-week fix.</p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-semibold text-foreground mb-4">Why does tennis elbow keep coming back?</h3>
          <p>A very common pattern is tennis elbow that seems to settle, then returns once normal activity resumes. The usual explanation isn't bad luck — it's that the pain eased before the tendon's load capacity was restored. Rest and reduced activity can calm symptoms, but they also decondition the tendon's tolerance for load; they don't rebuild its capacity to handle gripping and lifting. Going back to the same demands without a structured strengthening process in between reproduces the original overload. This is why an assessment-based approach that includes progressive loading — not just symptom relief — tends to give more lasting results than rest alone.</p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-semibold text-foreground mb-4">Do I have to stop my sport or training completely?</h3>
          <p>Not necessarily — and for many people, complete rest isn't realistic or the most effective path. What usually matters more than stopping entirely is modifying the specific movements that aggravate the tendon while continuing everything else: adjusting grip, temporarily reducing load on wrist-extension movements, changing equipment (for racquet sports, a more flexible frame or different string tension), or shifting training toward areas that don't load the affected tendon while it recovers. A thorough assessment helps identify which specific movements are driving the problem, so modifications can be targeted rather than guesswork.</p>

          <hr class="my-12 border-border" />

          <h2 class="text-3xl font-bold text-foreground mb-6">What actually helps with tennis elbow?</h2>
          
          <ul class="space-y-4">
            <li><strong>Progressive loading exercise</strong> is the best-supported core treatment. Eccentric exercise — where the tendon lengthens under tension — is described as a first-line conservative treatment, improving pain and grip strength (Yoon et al., Journal of Clinical Medicine, 2021). Isometric exercise (holding a position without movement) is often used earlier, when the tendon is too irritated for more demanding loading.</li>
            <li><strong>Treating the whole arm, not just the elbow.</strong> More recent work argues against treating local eccentric exercise as the only answer, recommending progressive loading that considers the wrist, forearm, and shoulder together as a connected chain (Stasinopoulos, Journal of Clinical Medicine, 2022) — because poor mechanics elsewhere in the arm can force extra strain through the elbow tendon.</li>
            <li><strong>Manual therapy: helpful, with honest limits.</strong> A 2024 Cochrane review found low-certainty evidence that manual therapy and prescribed exercise may slightly reduce pain and disability at the end of treatment, though the benefit may be modest and not sustained without continued exercise (Wallis et al., Cochrane Review, 2024). In practice, that makes hands-on treatment a useful part of a plan — most valuable combined with loading and motor re-education — rather than a standalone fix.</li>
            <li><strong>Rest has a role, but shouldn't be the only strategy.</strong> Reducing or modifying the aggravating activity helps early on, but rest alone deconditions load tolerance rather than rebuilding it — which is exactly why tennis elbow so often returns once activity resumes.</li>
          </ul>

          <hr class="my-12 border-border" />

          <h2 class="text-3xl font-bold text-foreground mb-6">How We Treat Tennis Elbow</h2>
          <h3 class="text-2xl font-semibold text-foreground mb-4">Following the chain from elbow to neck</h3>
          
          <p>Eva's assessment is more extensive than most patients expect. She tests not just the elbow and forearm, but also the wrist, shoulder, and cervical spine — because effective treatment depends on looking for contributing factors throughout the functional chain, rather than focusing solely on the site of pain.</p>

          <ul class="space-y-4 mt-6">
            <li><strong>Neurodynamic assessment & soft-tissue mobilisation — evaluating radial nerve mobility from neck to hand.</strong> Eva evaluates radial nerve mobility and tissue-interface sensitivity along its pathway — from the neck through the arm to the hand. Where the nerve is involved, gentle neurodynamic and soft-tissue techniques help restore normal nerve sliding through the forearm tissues — always alongside restoring functional movement and motor control, since nerve gliding on its own is not a stand-alone cure.</li>
            <li><strong>Soft-tissue & fascial work — settling overactive muscle.</strong> The forearm extensors and supinator often become hypertonic and overactive when deeper stabilisers (such as the deep neck flexors and lower scapular stabilisers) are inhibited — the kind of muscle imbalance described in Janda's work. Soft-tissue and fascial techniques are used to inhibit that overactive, hypertonic tissue so the area can be re-educated toward better movement patterns — not simply to "increase blood flow." Particular attention goes to the extensor carpi radialis brevis (the most commonly affected) and the tissues around the radial nerve tunnel.</li>
            <li><strong>Progressive tendon loading — evidence-based rehabilitation, not rest.</strong> The evidence supports graded loading over rest to rebuild load capacity (Yoon et al., 2021). Eva prescribes a loading program matched to your stage: pain-free isometric holds early on (which also have a pain-relieving effect), progressing to eccentric loading and functional, activity-specific strengthening.</li>
            <li><strong>Cervical & thoracic management — addressing contributing regional factors.</strong> When nerve sensitivity or stabilisation traces to the neck or upper body, manual therapy and movement work address the contributing regional factors — cervical mobility, thoracic mobility, and scapular positioning and centration — that local elbow treatment alone would miss. This reflects a core principle of the functional (Prague School) tradition: the site of pain is often not its source. <a href="https://scandinavianclinic.com" class="text-primary hover:underline">Read about neck pain treatment →</a></li>
          </ul>

          <hr class="my-12 border-border" />

          <h2 class="text-3xl font-bold text-foreground mb-6">What to Expect</h2>
          <h3 class="text-2xl font-semibold text-foreground mb-4">Tendon recovery takes time — the right treatment makes it shorter</h3>
          
          <p>Tendons heal and remodel slowly because of their limited blood supply, so expect recovery over weeks rather than days. Many patients with uncomplicated tennis elbow improve significantly within four to eight sessions combined with consistent home exercise.</p>
          <p>When nerve involvement or spinal dysfunction is a significant contributor, addressing those factors often helps unlock progress in cases that have stalled under local elbow care alone.</p>
          <p>Eva will be clear about expected timelines and adjust the plan as you progress. Home exercises — particularly the loading program — are critical: rebuilding a tendon's load capacity depends on progressive loading, and your consistency between sessions directly affects recovery speed.</p>

          <hr class="my-12 border-border" />

          <div class="grid md:grid-cols-2 gap-12 items-center my-12">
            <div>
              <h2 class="text-3xl font-bold text-foreground mb-6">Why Scandinavian Clinic</h2>
              <h3 class="text-xl font-semibold text-foreground mb-4">A full-chain approach to tennis elbow</h3>
              <ul class="space-y-4">
                <li><strong>Swedish-trained orthopedic manual therapy.</strong> Eva's training includes neurodynamic assessment of the upper limb — used to evaluate the nerve component that can keep tennis elbow going.</li>
                <li><strong>Czech-trained DNS therapist.</strong> When posture and shoulder-blade mechanics contribute, DNS helps restore trunk stabilisation and dynamic joint centration — the postural foundation that takes load off the elbow. <a href="https://scandinavianclinic.com" class="text-primary hover:underline">Learn more</a></li>
                <li><strong>Beyond the elbow.</strong> When tennis elbow hasn't responded to bracing, rest, or an injection, the driver is often not in the elbow alone. Eva's full-chain assessment looks for the contributing factors that other treatments may have missed.</li>
                <li><strong>Covered by most insurance plans.</strong> As a Registered Massage Therapist, Eva's treatments are eligible for extended health benefits.</li>
              </ul>
              <div class="mt-6">
                <a href="https://scandinavianclinic.com" class="text-primary font-medium hover:underline">See all conditions Eva treats</a>
              </div>
            </div>
            <div>
              <img src={imgEvaConsultation} alt="Eva Andersson consultation" class="w-full rounded-xl shadow-lg" />
            </div>
          </div>

          <hr class="my-12 border-border" />

          <h2 class="text-3xl font-bold text-foreground mb-8">Frequently asked questions</h2>
          
          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div>
              <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-foreground mb-2">Can you get tennis elbow without playing tennis?</h3>
              <p>Yes — most people who develop it have never played tennis. Weightlifting, climbing, painting, plumbing, carpentry, computer-mouse use, and repetitive kitchen tasks are all common causes.</p>
            </div>
            
            <div>
              <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-foreground mb-2">Is tennis elbow an inflammation?</h3>
              <p>Not really, at least not in the way people assume. It's now understood as a degenerative tendinopathy — a failed-healing, wear-type change in the tendon — rather than an actively inflamed one. That's why "rest and anti-inflammatories" alone often isn't enough, and why gradually rebuilding the tendon's load capacity matters.</p>
            </div>
            
            <div>
              <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-foreground mb-2">Why is my tennis elbow taking so long to heal?</h3>
              <p>Tendons heal slowly because of a limited blood supply, and a degenerative tendon needs time and the right load to rebuild. Established cases often take several months to a year or more — considerably longer than general health resources sometimes suggest.</p>
            </div>
            
            <div>
              <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-foreground mb-2">Why does tennis elbow keep coming back after it feels better?</h3>
              <p>Pain often eases before the tendon's load capacity has been restored. Returning to the same gripping or lifting without a structured strengthening process reproduces the strain.</p>
            </div>
            
            <div>
              <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-foreground mb-2">Do I need to completely stop my sport or gym training?</h3>
              <p>Usually not. Modifying the specific aggravating movement — grip, load, or equipment — while continuing other activity is often more effective and realistic than complete rest.</p>
            </div>
            
            <div>
              <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-foreground mb-2">Does massage therapy help with tennis elbow?</h3>
              <p>Hands-on treatment can help settle overactive muscle and support recovery in the short term, particularly combined with a loading program, though the evidence supports it as one part of a broader plan rather than a standalone fix (Wallis et al., 2024).</p>
            </div>
            
            <div>
              <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-foreground mb-2">What's the difference between eccentric and isometric exercise?</h3>
              <p>Isometric exercise (holding a position without movement) is often used early, when the tendon is too irritated for more demanding work; eccentric exercise (controlled lengthening under tension) is a well-supported next step for actively rebuilding tendon capacity (Yoon et al., 2021).</p>
            </div>
          </div>

          <div class="bg-accent/10 p-8 rounded-2xl text-center my-16 border border-accent/20">
            <h2 class="text-3xl font-bold text-foreground mb-4">Schedule Your Visit</h2>
            <h3 class="text-xl font-medium text-muted-foreground mb-6">Book your appointment for tennis elbow treatment</h3>
            <p class="mb-8 max-w-2xl mx-auto">If your tennis elbow keeps coming back or hasn't responded to rest and bracing, the problem may extend beyond the tendon. Book an RMT assessment to look at the full picture.</p>
            <p class="mb-8 max-w-2xl mx-auto">Most extended health plans in BC cover Registered Massage Therapy. For work-related (WorkSafeBC / WCB) cases, please call and speak with Eva directly to arrange — <a href="tel:+16049264883" class="hover:text-primary transition-colors">(604) 926-4883</a>.</p>
            <a href="https://booking.scandinavianclinic.com" class="booking-link inline-block bg-accent hover:bg-accent/90 text-accent-foreground font-semibold py-4 px-8 rounded-lg shadow-lg transition-all hover:scale-105 mb-4">Book Your Appointment</a>
            <p class="text-sm text-muted-foreground">Suite 202 – 101 West 16th Street, North Vancouver · <a href="tel:+16049264883" class="hover:text-primary transition-colors">(604) 926-4883</a></p>
          </div>

          {/* REFERENCES */}
          <div class="mt-16 pt-8 border-t border-border/50 text-sm">
            <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-foreground mb-4">References</h3>
            <ol class="list-decimal pl-5 space-y-2 text-muted-foreground/80">
              <li>Yoon SY, Kim YW, Shin IS, et al. The beneficial effects of eccentric exercise in the management of lateral elbow tendinopathy: a systematic review and meta-analysis. <em>Journal of Clinical Medicine</em>. 2021;10(17):3968.</li>
              <li>Stasinopoulos D. Stop using the eccentric exercises as the gold standard treatment for the management of lateral elbow tendinopathy. <em>Journal of Clinical Medicine</em>. 2022;11(5):1325.</li>
              <li>Wallis JA, Bourne AM, Jessup RL, et al. Manual therapy and exercise for lateral elbow pain. <em>Cochrane Database of Systematic Reviews</em>. 2024;(5):CD013042.</li>
              <li>Vavken P. Differential diagnosis of lateral elbow pain. <em>Praxis</em>. 2017;106(1):29–36.</li>
            </ol>
            <p class="mt-4 text-muted-foreground/80">Background reading (Prague School / functional tradition): Lewit K, <em>Manipulative Therapy: Musculoskeletal Medicine</em>; Page P, Frank C, Lardner R, <em>Assessment and Treatment of Muscle Imbalance: The Janda Approach</em>; Kobesová A, Kolář P, <em>Developmental kinesiology (DNS)</em>.</p>
            <p class="mt-8 text-xs italic text-muted-foreground">Written by Eva Andersson, RMT · Last updated July 2026</p>
          </div>

        </div>
      </div>
    </main>
    
    <footer class="bg-muted py-12 mt-12 border-t border-border">
      <div class="container mx-auto px-4 text-center text-muted-foreground text-sm space-y-4">
        <p>Suite 202 – 101 West 16th Street, North Vancouver, BC V7M 1T3 | <a href="tel:+16049264883" class="hover:text-primary transition-colors">(604) 926-4883</a></p>
        <p>Covered by most extended health insurance plans.</p>
        <p><a href="https://scandinavianclinic.com" class="hover:text-primary underline transition-colors">scandinavianclinic.com</a></p>
